"""What the translator and the engines were measured to do that the rows' families do not state
(REQ-2236). Each refusal the translator makes of a call a grid row matches is stated in the row
(THE REFUSAL LIVES IN THE GRID): a row that keeps the old table's form (trino.KEPT) hands the call
to the old table, which refuses by name where it has no form (function_macros.FunctionRefused,
0A000 ``F() has no form on <dialect>``); a rewrite function that answers nothing of a call does
the same; a native row's call can be refused by name before any row is read
(provisa.transpiler.engine_refusals); and a rewrite function or a literal's reading refuses a
value with its own message.

It also holds what the engines were measured to do at the edge values of a Conformance (functions)
run where a row states no limitation (:data:`BOUNDED`): the values an engine does not hold as
PostgreSQL does, and the SQLSTATE it raises at them, or that it answers otherwise there.

:data:`REFUSED` holds the rules, kept or native on a dialect, whose every call is refused there by
name: there the row's form is that refusal. :data:`LIMITED` holds the rest: there the row keeps its
form and lists the refusal as a limitation. A message's ``{dialect}`` is the dialect's name. Each
entry carries a call refused on each of its dialects, which a unit test holds it to.

scripts/conformance/gen_measured.py writes the tables between the markers from a
Conformance (functions) run: each statement the run made of an overload, transpiled to each
dialect with the rows as their families write them."""

